% ============================================================================
% comma-rule — three named comma rules and what each actually says to do
% (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A comma is not an end-of-sentence mark like the period, question mark, or
% exclamation point tabled in the sibling `end-punctuation-mark.adj` -- it
% is a MID-sentence mark, and which rule applies depends on WHY it's there:
% separating items in a list, joining two independent clauses in front of a
% coordinating conjunction, or setting off the name of the person being
% spoken to. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? comma_rule(comma_in_a_series, $D)
%       % use_commas_to_separate_elements_in_a_list_of_more_than_two_elements
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `comma_rule(rule, description)` carrying the source citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns
% the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a comma-related term that is not one of these three. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the rule it
% names:
%
%     ? comma_rule($R, set_off_the_name_with_commas_when_addressing_another_person_by_name)
%       % comma_with_direct_address
%
% The three comma rules, as a little truth table:
%
%     rule                        description                                                                        quote (verbatim)
%     ---------------------------  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     comma_in_a_series             use_commas_to_separate_elements_in_a_list_of_more_than_two_elements                 "When you have a list that contains more than two elements, use commas to separate them."
%     comma_before_but              use_a_comma_before_but_when_it_is_joining_two_independent_clauses                   "Use a comma before the coordinating conjunction but if it is joining two independent clauses:"
%     comma_with_direct_address     set_off_the_name_with_commas_when_addressing_another_person_by_name                 "When addressing another person by name, set off the name with commas."
%
% Provenance: quoted from Grammarly's "Rules for Using Commas,
% With Examples" article. The original authoring note said the quotes were
% "WebFetch-verified twice" and that each is a "complete, standalone rule
% sentence"; the raw-page measurement below supersedes it, and the "but"
% sentence ends in a colon, not a period (see below).
% `trust consensus` — Grammarly is a reputable secondary reference, the
% same tier the sibling `end-punctuation-mark.adj`/`capitalization-rule.adj`
% (the other libraries in this directory) already use for their Grammarly
% citations.
%
% Honest abstention on `oxford_comma`: a real, well-known term the SAME
% page discusses under its own heading, but its own rule sentence bundles
% the placement rule together with a caveat about its optionality ("the
% final comma ... is optional") rather than stating one clean single fact
% like the three tabled here. So a recall on it ABSTAINS rather than
% inventing a description the source's own sentence doesn't cleanly state.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SENTENCE (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). The envelope
% used to be the series row's own sentence, the primary source of all three
% answers. Each quoted sentence above names its rule and what it says to do,
% so every row now takes its own as a `source`. The envelope is now the
% article's framing sentence, which names no rule:
%
%     "There are lots of rules about comma usage, and often the factors that
%      determine whether you should use one are quite subtle."
%
% Measured 2026-09-15 on the raw page (HTTP 200; a nonsense path on the same
% host returns 404), with `<script>` and `<style>` data set aside, inline
% tags removed without a space, and only ASCII whitespace collapsed: the
% three row sentences and the envelope each occur exactly once, each inside a
% `<p>`. (The page's embedded script data repeats them; not counted.)
% Every row's page is the envelope's, so no row restates a `locator` line or
% `trust` (ADJ-TABLES §4).
%
% THE "BUT" QUOTE WAS NOT VERBATIM. The truth table ended it with a period;
% the page ends it with a COLON, which introduces an example pair. In the
% period form it occurs zero times. The quote above and the row now carry
% the page's colon form: the sentence is a whole instruction naming the rule
% and its action, even though its punctuation leads into the examples.
% ============================================================================

table comma_rule {
    columns rule, description

    row (comma_in_a_series, use_commas_to_separate_elements_in_a_list_of_more_than_two_elements) {
        source "When you have a list that contains more than two elements, use commas to separate them."
    }
    row (comma_before_but, use_a_comma_before_but_when_it_is_joining_two_independent_clauses) {
        source "Use a comma before the coordinating conjunction but if it is joining two independent clauses:"
    }
    row (comma_with_direct_address, set_off_the_name_with_commas_when_addressing_another_person_by_name) {
        source "When addressing another person by name, set off the name with commas."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE WAS THE SERIES ROW'S OWN SENTENCE, the primary source of
    % all three answers. It is now a framing sentence that names no rule.
    % Every row overrides it, so it reaches no answer.
    source "There are lots of rules about comma usage, and often the factors that determine whether you should use one are quite subtle."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/punctuation-capitalization/comma/"
    trust consensus
}
